# FinDraft — Draft Financial Statements Engine (FRS 102, Section 1A first)
# Deterministic numbers. Rules v7.5 — product decisions applied: members' accounts in scope; LLM uses v1.1; aggregate() stays in the production path; namespace findraft/.
# Numeric thresholds and statutory figures live in pack data, never in these rules.

## 1. PROJECT IDENTITY
You are building FinDraft: trial balance + source documents in → compliant FRS 102 Section 1A members'-accounts drafts out (statement of financial position, income statement, small-company directors' report, notes from 10 note templates N0–N9, approval and audit-exemption statements), for Irish accounting practices, UK pack as fast-follow. CORE PRINCIPLE: deterministic engine for numbers. The LLM never touches a number, mapping, or reconciliation result.

## 2. STACK (fixed — do not substitute)
Backend: FastAPI (Python 3.11+, async SQLAlchemy 2.0, Pydantic v2) · PostgreSQL 15+ · Celery + Redis · object storage: MinIO in local development, AWS S3 (eu-west-1) in production · React 18 + TypeScript + Tailwind + Vite · TanStack React Query for server state only; local UI state uses useState/useContext — never Redux/Zustand/SWR · WeasyPrint (Jinja2 HTML) for PDF · python-docx for Word export · OpenAI API (uses listed in §8 only) · Clerk JWT. Never introduce a second backend framework, ORM, state manager, or meta-framework.

## 3. ARCHITECTURE RULES
Directory roles: app layer (FastAPI routers+services) · engine/ (pure Python: mapping, reconciliation, statement generation, rounding, validation — NO DB or HTTP imports) · content/<pack>/ (pack data) · web/ (React) · tests/. All of these live under findraft/ inside the Kastree repo (DECIDED v6.6) — do not create other top-level directories.
REUSE RULE: before writing a new function, check whether an equivalent already exists in Product 1's codebase and extend it. Never duplicate auth, classification, or file-handling logic. Mapping DECIDED (v6.6): Product 1 OWNS mapping SUGGESTIONS (keyword scoring with the FinDraft exclusions ported and tested there). engine/mapping.aggregate() STAYS IN THE PRODUCTION STATEMENT PATH — it reclassifies overdrawn cash accounts before totalling so accounts never net against each other; do not route statements around it. A contract test enforces that Product 1's output uses the engine's canonical line names (BANK_OVERDRAFT, FA_INTANGIBLE_AMORT, …). Namespace DECIDED (v6.6): FinDraft lives at findraft/ inside the Kastree repo — never a parallel root tree. Never merge, replace, or duplicate either mapping module without instruction.
engine/ functions are pure over plain dataclasses; services inject data. Money: Decimal(15,2), 0.01 tolerance, no floats. Every figure materialised with lineage — no compute-at-render money.
Target: standard-specific text and structure live in content/<pack>/<version>/ so a new standard needs zero engine changes. Accepted exception until the pack-schema decision: engine/statements.py owns statement labels, line order and the 1A.6A balance-sheet compliance statement (kept identical to the pack's statements.py by test); content/*/statements.py and policies.py are not read by the engine. Do not migrate this unless asked. engine/lines.py is the ONE registry of canonical lines and statement groups: every sum (statements, notes context, review rules, mapping exclusions) iterates it — never hand-type a list of lines anywhere else.

## 4. HARD GATES & INVARIANTS (tests must enforce)
prior_year_validated=true before reconciliation (else BLOCKED) · TB debits=credits EXACTLY · BS balances EXACTLY (same comparison as articulates; rounding belongs only to presentation) · FA roll-forward ties · RE roll-forward ties · comparatives == validated prior year (prior year stored as canonical-line balances; comparatives rendered by the same builders via prior_from_mapped) · bank rec vs manual input · mapping confidence: 100 = auto-confirm (reachable ONLY via a human-confirmed prior-year exact match); heuristics are capped at 79 and prior-code matches score 60, so 80–99 never occurs today — do not build UI for it; 50–79 = active choice; <50 = review queue · confirmed mappings LOCKED (no LLM/background mutation) · LLM output never parsed into a number field · a review rule that cannot be evaluated, or whose input is missing, reports CRITICAL and blocks FINAL · disclosure answers are tri-state (yes / no / unanswered): an unanswered fact is never treated as "no" — a note needing it is included as pending and V-DISC-001 (CRITICAL) blocks FINAL until every applicable question is answered · facts derivable from the TB (e.g. director's loans, via aggregate() lineage) are derived, never asked.
ERROR FALLBACK: if any LLM call fails or times out, fall through to the safe deterministic state — mapping goes to needs-review, extraction goes to manual entry — never silently guess, retry at most once, never block the request thread.

## 5. MVP BEHAVIOURS
IN MVP: (1) rounding flags with chart-of-accounts deep-link (engine/rounding.py) · (2) draft version locking · (3) report options & styles · (4) in-place note editing + text blocks.
NOT in MVP (DECIDED v6.6): roll forward (A12) and cash-flow working column (A8) → v1.1; LLM features → v1.1; firm→client report-option inheritance → Week 14 fast-follow. Build these only when instructed.
DEFERRED to v1.1 (do not build): right-click context menu, anchored cell comments with Resolve workflow, cross-client disclosure import/export.

## 6. CONTENT PACK CONTRACT (content/frs102-1a-ie/2024.09/*)
Note template JSON (code = file stem): {title, bodyTemplate, sourceRef, includeWhen, jurisdiction, needsInput, tableSpec?} plus note-specific extras. Note 2 builds the FA movement grid. tableSpec.lines_from lists canonical line names only (each must be a presented line); every includeWhen, including nested ones, is validated at load. Two DIFFERENT compliance statements are required — never merge or remove either: the 1A.6A statement on the SoFP above the directors' signatures (Ireland: s.324(4A) CA 2014), and note N1's statement of compliance with Section 1A (1AD.3; s.291(7)). policies.py: 12 toggleable blocks {id, title, appliesWhen, body}; wording is fixed and subject to qualified sign-off — never generate or rewrite it. disclosure-checklist.json: Appendix D items 1AD.1–1AD.55 {id, topic, note, statuteRef, statuteRefStatus, includeWhen?, evidence?}; tests/test_checklist_integrity.py enforces that every 'verified' item carries evidence whose quote contains its citation — never weaken that test or relabel an item to pass it. pack.json: {pack, standard, section, version, effective, effective_from, jurisdiction}; there is no thresholds block yet. UK pack mirrors with Appendix C (1AC) refs.

## 7. UI/UX RULES
Review dashboard red/amber/green; every issue has Resolve → deep-link to the exact screen. Statement preview: click a figure → evidence drawer showing contributing TB lines. Rounding flags amber with chart-of-accounts link. Locked drafts show a create-new-version flow, never silent mutation. Loading skeletons; empty states with next action; optimistic UI only for non-financial edits.

## 8. LLM BOUNDARIES & EXECUTION
MVP uses NO LLM calls (DECIDED v6.6): prior-year figures come from a manual entry form (plus a 'first financial period' path with no comparatives), and 50–79 mapping candidates go to the human review queue. (a) prior-year PDF extraction and (b) mapping tie-break are DEFERRED TO v1.1 — when built, extraction output is schema-validated before display (untrusted input). Accounting-policy wording comes from policies.py, never from the LLM. v1.1 principle, stated honestly: no LLM-produced number reaches a statement without human confirmation, and extracted totals must cross-cast (e.g. net assets = equity) before a human sees them. Forbidden: everything else.
EXECUTION: all LLM calls run as Celery background jobs, never in the request thread. Tie-break cache keyed by (tenant_id, normalised account-name pattern) — per practice, never shared across practices. Stateless calls, no training; prompt+output hash logged in audit_trail. No client data reaches any API before the OpenAI DPA and EU data-residency review are signed off (v1.1 precondition).

## 9. AUTH, MULTI-TENANCY & SCHEMA
Reuse Product 1's Clerk authentication and RLS-scoped multi-tenancy — no new auth system or tenancy model. New tables carry company_id foreign keys into the EXISTING clients/companies schema — never a parallel company structure. Lock, FINAL, adjustments and pack pinning are role-checked server-side, never by hiding UI.

## 10. QUALITY BAR
ruff + mypy --strict everywhere. The engine's existing typing backlog (94 mypy --strict errors at v7.5) is fixed as a Week 1 task, behind the full test suite with no behaviour change; until then fix only lines you change for a tested reason. Name tests by behaviour, never by release. Tests: golden-file renders; one pass+fail test per validation rule; the full suite (python -m unittest discover -s tests -t .) must stay green. Playwright happy path green. One logical change per Alembic migration; never edit shipped migrations.

## 11. MVP GUARDRAILS (flag, don't build)
iXBRL, accounting-software APIs, OCR, cash-flow STATEMENT (the directors' REPORT is IN SCOPE for the members'-accounts set), FRS 105/101, consolidations, anomaly ML, client emails, LLM features (v1.1). BUT keep xbrl_concept_id, pack architecture, includeWhen, lineage — Phase 2 depends on all four.

## 12. DEFINITION OF DONE (per feature)
Migration → engine code + tests → router + Pydantic schema → React UI with states → audit entry → validation rules updated if money-affecting → disclosure checklist updated if a note changed → report-options defaults updated if a statement changed.

## 13. PACKS & VERSION PINNING
Two independent choices at client setup: JURISDICTION (IE | UK) and STANDARD (frs102-1a | frs102-full | frs105). Pack identity = <standard>-<jurisdiction>, versioned by pack.json "version" (e.g. frs102-1a-ie @ 2024.09). A new FRC edition = a NEW pack version in its own immutable directory content/<pack>/<version>/ (engine/pack.py pack_dir() resolves a pinned draft to exactly those files) — never edit a live pack in place. year_ends and draft_versions record pack_id + pack_version at creation and stay pinned; pinning refuses a period starting before the pack's effective_from. Packs govern presentation, formats, thresholds and disclosures; classification is not pack-specific.

## 14. CHANGE SAFETY
engine/ and content/ are audited. Change them only to fix a bug demonstrated by a failing test you add first. Never reformat, rename, or "clean up" engine code.
Never modify, skip, or delete an existing test to make a change pass. If a test blocks you, stop and explain why.
If a request conflicts with these rules or the spec, stop and ask — do not guess.

## 15. DEPENDENCIES
Do not add any package not already declared without asking first. Never invent package names or APIs; if unsure a function exists, search the codebase.
No new infrastructure (queues, caches, databases, services) without an explicit request.

## 16. SCOPE DISCIPLINE
Implement exactly what is asked. No speculative abstractions, base classes, plugin systems, or config options "for later".
One feature per change; touch the fewest files possible and list every file you changed.

## 17. DATA & TENANCY
Every new table: tenant_id NOT NULL + an RLS policy + a test proving tenant A cannot read tenant B's rows. RLS (Week 1, the existential risk): the app connects as a role that does NOT own the tables and has NOBYPASSRLS; FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on every table anyway; every policy is USING and WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id', true)::uuid), which matches zero rows when the context is unset; the context is set in ONE place per transaction with set_config('app.tenant_id', …, true) (e.g. SQLAlchemy after_begin), from a ContextVar filled by auth from the VERIFIED Clerk organisation claim — never from a path or body parameter; workers use the same helper; the cross-tenant CI test runs under the production role, never a superuser.
Tenant-owned rows reference companies by a composite foreign key (tenant_id, company_id) → companies(tenant_id, id), with UNIQUE(tenant_id, id) on companies, so a row can never pair one tenant's id with another tenant's company.
asyncpg behind PgBouncer in transaction mode: connect_args statement_cache_size=0; size pools so (tasks × (pool_size + max_overflow)) stays below max_connections minus headroom. Cache Clerk JWKS, refreshing on an unknown kid. CORS: explicit allowlist of the Kastree frontend origin.
Money is Decimal end-to-end: NUMERIC(15,2) in Postgres, strings in JSON APIs, never JavaScript number arithmetic on money.
Celery tasks receive IDs only — never client data in the payload — and re-establish tenant context themselves.
Each TB upload is a new immutable TB version; a draft records the TB version it was built from, and a re-import creates a new draft version — never mutates an existing draft.
The client entity record (legal name, CRO number, registered office, directors, secretary, financial year, average employees) is captured once and feeds note N0, the directors' report and the approval page — never retyped per draft.
Derived output is a snapshot, not tables: inputs stay relational and immutable (TB version, CONFIRMED mappings only, adjustments, entity record, disclosure answers, pack id + version); each computation's statements, notes and check results are ONE immutable JSONB document keyed by sha256(inputs) + engine SHA + pack version, recomputed on every change (the engine runs in milliseconds). Unconfirmed mapping suggestions never reach aggregate().
Draft concurrency (Week 10): draft_versions.row_version with optimistic concurrency (zero rows updated → 409); SELECT … FOR UPDATE on the draft inside adjust, recompute and finalise; a database trigger refuses INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on adjustments, note overrides, text blocks and mappings when the parent draft is LOCKED or FINAL; FINAL is one transaction that writes the snapshot, inputs_sha256, engine SHA and pack version, then flips the status — FINAL output is never recomputed. Upload, adjust and finalise require an Idempotency-Key.
Rendering: PDF and DOCX render in a separate worker process with a memory cap and timeout, never inside an async handler; the API returns a job id. Reuse Kastree's Celery if it exists; otherwise a Postgres job table with SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED — do not add Redis/Celery for this alone. Call the engine directly (pure CPU, milliseconds) — no async or thread-pool wrapper.
Review rules always receive the draft's pinned pack: engine.pack.pack_dir(pack_id, version) / "review-rules.json" — there is no default.
UPLOADS (Week 2 acceptance): cap file size; verify the REAL file type (never trust the extension); reject macro-enabled workbooks (.xlsm); guard against zip bombs (cap decompressed size); per-practice storage prefixes/keys; parse spreadsheets in the worker, never the API process (openpyxl read_only=True, hard row and column caps); escape formula-leading characters (= + - @) on import as well as export, since account names flow into PDFs, DOCX and CSVs — uploads are the main outside-data entry point.
AUDIT TRAIL: append-only for real — REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE from the app role, plus a per-row prev_hash chain so tampering is detectable.
RETENTION vs ERASURE (Week 12 acceptance): state the policy for statutory accounting-record retention periods vs GDPR erasure — which wins, and when; never ship a delete path that removes records the practice is legally required to keep.
